namespace TrueSpec.Core.Localization;

/// <summary>
/// The built-in language packs. English is the fallback for every key:
/// a language that misses a key shows the English text, never a blank.
/// German is the first additional language (v2.1.7): the navigation
/// rail and the Settings language card; v2.1.8 adds the whole Settings
/// page; v2.1.9 adds the Core diagnostic strings (plug-in status and
/// reasons, manifest errors, portable-toggle messages); v2.1.10 adds
/// the shared component detail view; v2.1.11 adds the Overview page;
/// v2.1.12 adds the History page; v2.1.16 adds the Report, Tests,
/// New Laptop Check and Crash History pages (phase 8).
/// French is the second additional language (v2.1.20, phase 1): the
/// navigation rail and the Settings language card, the same first
/// surface German phase 1 translated; v2.1.21 adds the whole Settings
/// page (phase 2), the same second surface German phase 2 translated;
/// v2.1.22 adds the Core diagnostic strings (phase 3), the same third
/// surface German phase 3 translated; v2.1.23 adds the shared
/// component detail view (phase 4), the same fourth surface German
/// phase 4 translated; v2.1.24 adds the Overview page (phase 5), the
/// same fifth surface German phase 5 translated; v2.1.25 adds the
/// History page (phase 6, the same sixth surface German phase 6
/// translated) plus the Fleet and New Laptop Check pages (the same
/// two surfaces German phase 8 translated); v2.1.26 adds the
/// Comparison, Crash History, Report and Tests pages (phase 7, the
/// same four surfaces German phase 8 translated). With phase 7 every
/// page is translated in French, and the French language card says
/// so. Measured data stays English by design (names, headlines, spec
/// labels and values, score explanations and verdict text), a later
/// slice. The French wording is the worker's own translation, not
/// reviewed by a native speaker (the same honest limitation as the
/// German wording). The translations use the standard terms
/// Windows itself uses in French (Stockage for Storage, Affichage
/// for Display, Démarrage for Startup, Paramètres for Settings).
/// Spanish is the third additional language (v2.1.27, phase 1): the
/// navigation rail and the Settings language card, the same first
/// surface German phase 1 and French phase 1 translated; v2.1.28
/// adds the whole Settings page (phase 2), the same second surface
/// German phase 2 and French phase 2 translated; v2.1.29 adds the
/// Core diagnostic strings (phase 3), the same third surface German
/// phase 3 and French phase 3 translated; v2.1.30 adds the shared
/// component detail view (phase 4), the same fourth surface German
/// phase 4 and French phase 4 translated; v2.1.31 adds the Overview
/// page (phase 5), the same fifth surface German phase 5 and French
/// phase 5 translated; v2.1.32 adds the History, Fleet, and Buyer
/// Check pages (phase 6), the same sixth surface German phase 6 and
/// French phase 6 translated; v2.1.33 adds the Comparison, Crash
/// History, Report and Tests pages (phase 7), the same four surfaces
/// German phase 8 and French phase 7 translated. With phase 7 every
/// page is translated in Spanish, and the Spanish language card says
/// so. Measured data stays English by design (names, headlines, spec
/// labels and values, score explanations and verdict text), a later
/// slice. The Spanish pack
/// covers the whole 523-key catalogue (the coverage test pins it: a
/// pack that misses a key fails), so later phases only translate
/// surfaces, never add keys. The Spanish wording is the worker's own translation, not reviewed by
/// a native speaker (the same honest limitation as the German and
/// French wording). The translations use the standard terms Windows
/// itself uses in Spanish (Almacenamiento for Storage, Pantalla for
/// Display, Inicio for Startup, Configuración for Settings).
/// The translations use the standard terms Windows itself uses in
/// German (Speicher for Storage, Anzeige for Display, Autostart for
/// Startup, Einstellungen for Settings), so a German-speaking buyer
/// meets familiar words, not invented ones. Brand names (TrueSpec) and
/// component abbreviations (CPU, RAM, GPU) are never translated.
/// Templates with placeholders ({0}, {1}) are formatted with the
/// invariant culture by StringLocalizer.GetFormatted: the numbers in
/// them keep the app's existing formatting, and the placeholder order
/// is part of the translation (German word order differs).
/// </summary>
public static class LanguagePacks
